{"input":{"content":"---\nname: rct2-coaster-contest-tactics\ndescription: What actually moves excitement in the RCT2 steel-twister (ride_type 51) building contest\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\nMulti-round RCT2 coaster contest via the `coaster` MCP tools. Measured results across rounds 3-4 (best so far: 7.13 excitement, 128 pieces):\n\n- A **second mid-ride chain lift** is the single biggest lever. Draining all height into one drop leaves the back half slow; re-lifting to ~320z mid-circuit doubled `num_drops` (7 -> 14) and pushed `average_speed` from ~148k to ~232k. Excitement went 6.60 -> 7.13.\n- **Airtime hops are cheap excitement.** Swapping plain `flat` runs for `flat_to_up_25, up_25_to_flat, flat_to_down_25, down_25_to_flat` (same footprint, net dz 0) took `total_air_time` 64 -> 84.\n- Inversions count per *element*, not per piece: a `corkscrew_up`+`corkscrew_down` pair = 1 inversion, same as one vertical loop.\n- Intensity is the binding constraint, not excitement. It sat at 9.4-10.2 in every good build. `max_positive_g` ~4.7 comes from the vertical loops and 60-degree pullouts and did NOT respond to swapping `banked_*_turn_3` for `banked_*_turn_5` (lateral G stayed 2.99).\n- Similarity to stock designs stayed ~0.36-0.41 with these layouts, safely under the 0.5 penalty threshold.\n\n**How to apply:** plan the elevation profile as lift -> drop -> elements -> *lift again* -> drop -> elements -> brakes. Track the cursor on paper using rotation R(x,y) = (y,-x) applied k times for facing k; `piece_geometry` only returns dir-0 deltas. Close the circuit with `left_turn_5` from (start_x+3, start_y-2) facing dir 1.\n","file_path":"/home/sandbox/.claude/projects/-workspace/memory/rct2-coaster-contest-tactics.md"},"kind":"tool","name":"Write","output":"File created successfully at: /home/sandbox/.claude/projects/-workspace/memory/rct2-coaster-contest-tactics.md","seq":40,"status":"completed"}
