{"kind":"text","seq":42,"text":"Round 4 progression, all tested and banked:\n\n| Build | Excitement | Intensity | Length | Drops | Inversions | Airtime |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| v1 (single lift, 56 pcs) | 6.10 | 6.07 | 401 | 5 | 2 | 22 |\n| v2 (bigger hills, 84 pcs) | 6.60 | 8.74 | 557 | 7 | 3 | 40 |\n| v3 (+ mid-ride lift, 118 pcs) | 6.94 | 9.37 | 770 | 10 | 4 | 52 |\n| v4 (+ 5th inversion, 128 pcs) | 7.09 | 9.88 | 851 | 12 | 5 | 64 |\n| **v5 (+ airtime hops)** | **7.13** | 10.17 | 853 | 14 | 5 | 84 |\n\nThe decisive change was a **second chain lift mid-circuit**: the first version dumped all its height into one drop and crawled through the back half (avg speed 148k). Re-lifting to 320z halfway restored speed to 232k and doubled the drop count. Swapping flat straights for bunny-hops then added 20 units of airtime for free.\n\nTwo caveats worth stating plainly: the winning build's intensity is **10.17, just over the ~10 guideline** — it still scored highest, so the penalty appears soft rather than a cliff, but I didn't have budget left to confirm whether a sub-10 variant would score better. I also tried widening the fast turns (3-tile → 5-tile) specifically to cut lateral G; it did nothing, lateral G stayed at 2.99, so that G is coming from the loops/corkscrews themselves, not the turns.\n\nBest coaster: a 128-piece dual-lift twister — 240-unit plunge into a vertical loop, two corkscrew pairs, a mid-ride re-lift into a second 208-unit drop with a second loop and corkscrew, then a bunny-hop run home — **7.13 excitement** (similarity 0.39, well under the 0.5 penalty)."}
